WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.411
A shop sends a card issuer an authorization for 12.50, across the network.

2
00:00:07.411 --> 00:00:12.313
Three parts can now fail on their own: the shop, the network, and the issuer.

3
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:14.895
The shop starts a two second timer.

4
00:00:14.895 --> 00:00:17.728
The timer runs out, and no reply has come.

5
00:00:17.728 --> 00:00:19.145
Where is the request?

6
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:21.402
It may have been lost on the way.

7
00:00:21.402 --> 00:00:24.671
The issuer never saw it, and the card is not charged.

8
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.571
It may be stuck in a queue.

9
00:00:26.571 --> 00:00:32.572
It reaches the issuer at five seconds, and the card is charged then, long after the shop gave up.

10
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.131
The issuer may have crashed before it charged the card.

11
00:00:36.131 --> 00:00:37.827
Nobody is left to charge it.

12
00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:41.215
Or the issuer is paused by a long garbage collection.

13
00:00:41.215 --> 00:00:46.857
The request waits in its buffer, and at five seconds the issuer wakes up and charges the card.

14
00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:52.919
The card may have been charged at 51 milliseconds, and the reply was lost on the way back.

15
00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.898
The reply may just be slow.

16
00:00:54.898 --> 00:00:58.885
It lands at five seconds, after the shop has already decided.

17
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.249
The shop sees the same thing in all six worlds: no reply.

18
00:01:03.249 --> 00:01:05.797
In two, the card was never charged.

19
00:01:05.797 --> 00:01:07.472
In two, it already was.

20
00:01:07.472 --> 00:01:08.855
In two, it will be.

21
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:11.170
Waiting longer doesn't settle it.

22
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:16.269
Whatever timeout you pick, a queued request or a paused issuer can act after it.

23
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.427
So a remote call has three outcomes: yes, no, and unknown.

24
00:01:20.427 --> 00:01:27.671
If the shop calls unknown a failure, the customer pays again, and in four worlds the card is charged twice.

25
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:34.669
The way out is to retry with the same idempotency key, an id the shop creates once for this payment.

26
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:42.102
Where the card was already charged, the issuer finds the key and sends back the stored answer, without charging again.

27
00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:44.727
Where it wasn't, the retry charges it once.

28
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:48.951
One charge in every world, and now the shop knows the outcome.
